Undoubtedly there are many more bugs/enhancement needed, but these are the ones I know about:


Cairo asserts on exit due to non-empty object hash (i.e. something didn't get disposed)

TextEdit control works but has issues
	Selection changes sometimes don't show up until the next repaint
	When scrolled, selecting text does not always highlight the correct range

Window repaint is inefficient
	After any Invalidate(), the entire window backing buffer is copied to the window.
	It should be limited to the inval rect.

Some Be apps set their initial window size to 0,0,0,0 and it gets resized correctly
	We now have a mechanism to fix this in the backend message handler
	for AS_WINDOW_CREATE, but we can't use it yet because it hangs the redraw semaphore.
	This should be tested on Terminal and Pairs since they used to do this trick.
	This will also be how we set Wayland window fFrame to (0,0) (see below).

Modal alerts are not fully modal on Wayland
	The alert stays front-most, but input/clicks are allow in the parent window

B_CMAP8 (and a few others) are not valid color spaces for drawing, due to lack of support in Cairo
	Should we auto-upgrade these color spaces to B_RGB32 at BBitmap creation to avoid issues, or
	would this just cause new ones as apps try to insert 8-bit bitmap data?
	You can still set or import a BBitmap's bits with data from B_CMAP8 or other
	unsupported-for-drawing color spaces -- it will get correctly converted to the BBitmap's
	supported color space.

Menu selection may cause a crash (Wayland)

Menus are not positioned exactly correctly (Wayland)

Menus are not positioned correctly until the parent window has been moved (Mac)

BFilePanel has parts that do not draw their background correctly

BFilePanel in save mode shows the filename to save in a too-small box

If you use an app for long enough it will eventually crash with a corrupt BMessage header
	Crash typically happens in UsePreferredTarget.  I've seen this on all platforms.
	Since this is almost completely unmodified Haiku code, I'm surprised they haven't seen this.

BRecentFilesList/BRecentFolderList are only partially implemented

Drag-n-Drop from external sources is not yet implemented on any backend

Toolbar menus hang the window when opened

BView RotateBy creates clipping issues
	This currently only affects rotated tab labels (TabView)

B_NOT_RESIZABLE incorrectly limits programmatic resizing of the window
	It should only prevent user resizing

Many APIs are just empty stubs or absent altogether
	get_mouse()
	BWindowStack
	Anything to do with printing
	BPicture, and BView methods dealing with BPicture
	BFont::GetGlyphShapes
	BDirectWindow

Columns - Column resizing has slight redraw issues (Mac)
Pulse - Far right side of window background doesn't draw (Mac)
Terminal - Does not open (Mac), scrolling back in the terminal produces artifacts (All)


The following were known issues from Cosmoe Classic before forking to this project:

entry_ref's only work if Name holds an absolute path or dot-relative path
	Converting from a BEntry or GetNextRef fills this out correctly, but considering how
	extensively entry_ref is used, it's certain this is causing issues somewhere.  I don't
	know of a specific problem though.

send_data() and receive_data() use a static, hard-coded 512 byte area to pass information
	The current implementation was a quick hack to get menus working, since they use this
	functionality extensively. I've never gone back and coded it to allocate memory dynamically.
	See src/system/kernel/thread.cpp
	To do this dynamically turns out to probably be harder than it's worth, as this is not used
	very often, and when it is, very small amounts of data are passed.

Unit tests aren't even close to 100% passing
	We are now synced with Haiku unit tests.  Unfortunately, they use a custom unit test library that
	is a pain to work with, since determining exactly where a test failed is very time-consuming.
	I know this will be hugely valuable in making Cosmoe more stable and compliant though.



Cosmoe portability caveats:

image_id is a pointer type, not an integer